<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sophia Palaiologina</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1455–<a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Zoe Palaiologina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sophia Palaiologina was a Byzantine princess, niece of the last emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in 1472. Brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, she came to Moscow through papal mediation, though hopes of drawing Moscow into union with Rome came to nothing. The marriage strengthened Muscovite claims to the Byzantine heritage, and her son became grand prince as Vasily III.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina.jpg" alt="Sophia Palaiologina" width="320" height="532" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Nikitin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S.paleolog_reconstruction03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
